https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0W_SGoBFJxs  (Capo 0)

TIP:  There's both an (Asus) and (Asus2):  (Asus) is (A) with the bottom finger slide forward one fret and (Asus2) is (A) without the bottom finger.  There's also an (E) and and (E4) with (E4) being (E) with your pinky added to the 3rd string/2nd fret just below the ring finger (or it's (A) but moved up one string-set).  But here's the the thing:  Play (A) and (E4) with your bottom three fingers.  So to move from (A) to (Asus) slide your pinky forward one fret.  You'll see why when you play the outro.   

TIP:  In the chorus there's a quick transition from (A) to (Asus2) and back to (A) in which you just lift off the treble string and then replace it.  If a bit much then just play the (A) straight through.  Also there's both a (G#m), played as a barre, and an (F#) which is played like an (F) up one fret but here's a trick:  from the (G#m) just hold form and slide down two frets.  It's such a quick transition that you don't need the full (F#).  Much easier.  
 
TIP:  There's a key change from A to G.  It's not complicated and the instrumental bridge gets you there with the transitional chords.

TIP:  The chords at the outro are a bit messy but are all simple additions of one finger to the major chord.  The only tricky bit is that the suspended chords come first.  For the untranposed (Cmaj7sus4) you just play a (C) and roll your index finger down to cover the bottom (f) and then lift up (if it doesn't transpose just play the transposed root chord).  You can fake it with the major chords just fine.  And the last set of (E)s are struck forcefully.}

            
